    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd.jpg" alt="Hendrik Verwoerd" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuid_Afrikaanse_premier_dr._H._Verwoerd,_Bestanddeelnr_911-1297_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Snikkers / Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was proclaimed on 31 May 1961, when the Union of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> ended its link with the British Crown. A republic was the long-standing goal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, in power since 1948, and of its prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>, who won a referendum of white voters on 5 October 1960 with about 52 per cent of the vote. When other Commonwealth members criticised apartheid at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> conference of March 1961, Verwoerd withdrew the application to remain a member, and the republic left the Commonwealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-r-swart">C. R. Swart</a>, the last governor-general, became the first state president, a largely ceremonial office, while executive power stayed with the prime minister. The new state was born amid crisis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> of 21 March 1960 had been followed by a state of emergency and the banning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a>, and in December 1961 the ANC and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-communist-party">South African Communist Party</a> launched an armed wing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> as its first commander. The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, and its rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> later declared illegal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sasol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sasol.jpg" alt="Sasol" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sasol">Sasol</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sasol_Corporate_Office_Johannesburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tom Corser, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For three decades the republic was organised by apartheid. The Population Registration Act classified every person by race, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> divided the cities, and pass laws controlled the movement of Africans, while the state removed an estimated 3.5 million people from their homes between 1960 and the early 1980s to enforce segregation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-education-act-1953">Bantu Education</a> gave African children deliberately inferior schooling. The economy grew fast in the 1960s on gold, other minerals and manufacturing, supported by state corporations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, electricity and synthetic fuels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasol">Sasol</a>, and the high gold price of the 1970s brought further income, but growth slowed in the 1980s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, strikes and debt. Since 1994 the democratic state has extended electricity, water, housing and social grants to millions of households, and a Black middle class has grown through education and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-economic-empowerment">Black Economic Empowerment</a> policies. Yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> remains one of the most unequal societies in the world, with unemployment above 30 per cent, a severe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiv-aids">HIV</a> epidemic in the 1990s and 2000s, high crime, and repeated power cuts, known as load-shedding, caused by failures at the state utility <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eskom">Eskom</a>. It is among the largest and most industrialised economies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with major mining, financial, agricultural and automotive sectors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war.jpg" alt="South African Border War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SABorder_War_Montage2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collage: User:Katangais. Individual constituent images used in the collage: User:Smikect, Chris Lofting, Allen Herweg..., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a> grew into the strongest military in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. Facing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> arms embargo, voluntary from 1963 and mandatory from 1977, the state built a large arms industry around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armscor">Armscor</a> and developed nuclear weapons, assembling six devices in the 1980s before dismantling them around 1990–1991, the first country to give up nuclear weapons it had built itself. From 1966 the army fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">South West African People’s Organisation</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a>, which spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> after 1975, when South African forces intervened in the Angolan civil war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> government and its Cuban allies. The fighting around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cuito-cuanavale">Cuito Cuanavale</a> in 1987–1988 ended without a decisive victory but pushed all sides towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york-accords">New York Accords</a> of December 1988, which led to Namibian independence in 1990 and the withdrawal of Cuban troops from Angola. The army and police were also used at home against township uprisings and, in cross-border raids, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">ANC</a> bases in neighbouring states. In 1994 the SADF merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> armies of the ANC and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">PAC</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> forces to form the South African National Defence Force, which has since served in peacekeeping missions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-vorster"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vorster.jpg" alt="John Vorster" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-vorster">John Vorster</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John-Vorster_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until 1984 the republic kept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster-style</a> parliament elected by white voters only. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Verwoerd</a> (1958–1966), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vorster">John Vorster</a> (1966–1978) and P. W. Botha (1978–1989) led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> governments that ruled through security laws, detention without trial and the banning of opponents. Grand apartheid assigned Africans to ten ethnic ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homelands</a>’, four of which, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transkei">Transkei</a> (1976), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a> (1977), Venda (1979) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a> (1981), were declared independent, although no other country recognised them. The constitution of 1983 created a tricameral parliament with chambers for whites, Coloured people and Indians but none for Africans, and an executive state president, an office Botha took in 1984. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a>, president from 1989, unbanned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">ANC</a> and freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> in 1990, and negotiations produced an interim constitution and the first democratic elections on 27 April 1994. The constitution adopted in 1996, with a Bill of Rights and a Constitutional Court, created a republic with an executive president elected by the National Assembly, nine provinces and eleven official languages. The ANC governed under Mandela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thabo-mbeki">Thabo Mbeki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kgalema-motlanthe">Kgalema Motlanthe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-ramaphosa">Cyril Ramaphosa</a> until it lost its majority in 2024 and formed a Government of National Unity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan.jpg" alt="Ronald Reagan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_President_Reagan_1981.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michael Evans, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> became the target of one of the largest international campaigns of the twentieth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> repeatedly condemned apartheid, and in 1973 the General Assembly adopted a convention declaring it a crime against humanity; sports, cultural and consumer boycotts and disinvestment campaigns spread across Western countries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comprehensive-anti-apartheid-act">United States Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act</a> of 1986 imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> over President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>’s veto. Within the country, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness</a> movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a>, who died in police custody in 1977, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-democratic-front-south-africa">United Democratic Front</a>, founded in 1983, created new forms of mass resistance. The negotiated transition of 1990–1994 made South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> a worldwide symbol of reconciliation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Mandela</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">de Klerk</a> shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1993. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Archbishop Desmond Tutu</a> from 1996, became a model studied by many societies emerging from conflict. Since 1994 South Africa has been an influential voice in African and global diplomacy, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brics">BRICS</a> group in 2011, hosting the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a> and in 2023 bringing a case against Israel under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide-convention">Genocide Convention</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>. Its writers, among them the Nobel laureates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadine-gordimer">Nadine Gordimer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-coetzee">J. M. Coetzee</a>, reached a world audience.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk.jpg" alt="F. W. de Klerk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F.W._de_Klerk_wait_to_speak_in_Philadelphia,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2011634246_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The apartheid state was transformed rather than overthrown. Township revolts from 1984, states of emergency from 1985, international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, the debt crisis of 1985 and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> that white minority rule could not last. On 2 February 1990 he announced the unbanning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">ANC</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">PAC</a> and the Communist Party, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Mandela</a> was released on 11 February after 27 years in prison. Negotiations at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-for-a-democratic-south-africa">Convention for a Democratic South Africa</a> (CODESA) and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multi-party-negotiating-forum">Multi-Party Negotiating Forum</a> survived violence between ANC supporters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inkatha-freedom-party">Inkatha Freedom Party</a> and the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-hani">Chris Hani</a> in April 1993. The elections of 27 April 1994 made Mandela president, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homelands</a> were reincorporated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> rejoined the Commonwealth. Since then the republic has been a constitutional democracy with regular elections, a free press and an independent judiciary, but it has struggled with corruption, notably the ‘state capture’ under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a> investigated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zondo-commission">Zondo Commission</a>, with the police killing of 34 striking miners at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marikana-massacre">Marikana</a> in 2012, and with crime and slow growth. In the 2024 election the ANC won about 40 per cent of the vote, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-ramaphosa">Ramaphosa</a> was re-elected president at the head of a coalition that includes the Democratic Alliance.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> moved from the most elaborate system of legal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> of the twentieth century to a negotiated multiracial democracy in 1994, and as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s largest economies and a leading voice of the Global South it remains central to the continent’s politics.</em></p>

